# Apps run in the users' Cloudflare, never in the maintainer's

The maintainer (iacomidavide@gmail.com, Cloudflare account "Davide Iacomi"
`40e62dc10f5e4fba580861b8398376aa`) must never pay for, host or store anything of the users.
This is permanent and applies to every app in `examples/apps`, to the catalog (`amable-apps`) and
to the control plane (`amable-cloud`).

- Never deploy an app, a Worker, a D1 / KV / R2 / Queue, a secret, a cron or a custom domain to the
  maintainer's account (no `wrangler deploy` of apps, no shared "apps host", no
  `apps.amabledata.com`). Never add CI steps or scripts that do.
- Each official app is a single module bundle (`scripts/bundle-app.mjs`) that the CRM deploys into
  the installation's **own** Cloudflare account on install: Worker `amable-app-<id>`, D1
  `amable-app-<id>-db`, its crons, its `workers.dev` address. Uninstall deletes them.
  - AmableData Cloud: the control plane deploys with the token the customer saved during the
    portal onboarding (`/v1/installation/apps/deploy`); a revoked token → `token_inactive` →
    the portal asks for a new one. The customer is never asked anything else.
  - Self-hosted: the owner creates the token (Workers Scripts + D1, Edit) from the prefilled link
    the first time; it is stored encrypted in the installation.
- An app needing more storage or another resource declares it in the manifest `deployment` block
  and the deployers create it in the user's account (add the permission to the prefilled token
  link and to the onboarding). Never "temporarily" put it on the maintainer's account.
- App data (installs, keys, tables) lives in the app's own D1 in the user's account. Signing keys
  are generated by the app on first request (`appKeysLoader`): no secrets to configure.
- Bundles are distributed for free as GitHub release assets of `Ratne/AmableData`
  (`node scripts/release-app.mjs <id> --publish`), verified by the `bundle_sha256` of the signed
  catalog. GitHub releases are the only thing the maintainer publishes.
- Third-party apps stay hosted by their developers, at their cost.
